{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":5,"timestamp":1779256100,"nonce":"tiTgCyrrSIiYWn3b7xD+ur0IBdI=","metadata":{"github-id":"LE_lADOK4fCvM8AAAABCz1oyc8AAAAF_kjFgA"},"added":["bug"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"timestamp":1779284506,"nonce":"flSuBu8C62kSLWILSjhMlpcLMVc=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABDCkoQA","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/144#issuecomment-4498991168"},"message":"## Audit + operator-side cross-check refutes the bidirectional-config root cause\n\nDid the audit suggested in this issue's last section (\"other bridged topics in `bizzyboat.yaml` should be audited for similar bidirectional-when-it-shouldn't-be patterns\"). Pulled the operator-side bag to cross-check. Result: the storm is real but the mechanism in this issue is wrong.\n\n### Audit: no bidirectional bridge config exists\n\n- `bizzyboat_project11/config/bizzyboat.yaml` (boat bridge, `name: bizzy`): ~40 outbound topics including `command_response: {source: marine/response}`.\n- `bizzyboat_project11/config/operator.yaml` (operator bridge, `name: operator`): **2 outbound topics**: `command: {source: /bizzy/marine/command}`, `joystick_helm: {source: /bizzy/piloting_mode/manual/helm}`.\n- `marine/response` does not appear in `operator.yaml` and never has — git log on that file shows commits `9985192`, `060bd08`, `c030136`, none of which reference `marine/response`.\n- The two `topics_list` blocks are **disjoint**: no topic is bridged in both directions.\n\nThe opening of this issue cites operator-side config `command_response: {source: marine/response}` mirroring the boat side. That config does not exist.\n\n### Operator-side bag confirms the storm but flips the direction\n\nPer-topic peaks from `/operator/udp_bridge/topic_statistics` and `/operator/udp_bridge/remotes/bizzy/topic_statistics` during 17:12–17:35 UTC:\n\n**Operator → boat (operator's outbound):**\n\n| source_topic → dest_topic | conn | peak msg/s | peak MB/s |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `/bizzy/marine/command` → same | wifi | 370,679 | 29.6 |\n| `/bizzy/marine/command` → same | vpn  | 370,679 | 29.6 |\n| `/bizzy/marine/command` (init) | —    |  99,317 |  8.0 |\n| `/bizzy/piloting_mode/manual/helm` | wifi | 38 | \u003c0.01 |\n\n**Boat → operator (bizzy's outbound, forwarded to operator):**\n\n| source_topic → dest_topic | conn | peak msg/s | peak MB/s |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `/bizzy/marine/response` → same | wifi | 48,991 | 2.74 |\n| `/bizzy/marine/response` → same | vpn  | 48,991 | 2.74 |\n| `/bizzy/marine/response` (init) | —    | 34,153 | 1.91 |\n| `/bizzy/marine/status/mission_manager` | wifi | 448 | 0.06 |\n\nThe dominant traffic is `/bizzy/marine/command` from operator at ~740k msg/s across wifi+vpn — **not** `marine/response` from boat. The 48k msg/s of `marine/response` is the reply, not the driver. The amplification ratio (operator out / boat ack) is ~7.6×.\n\n### Likely real mechanism\n\nThe retransmission loop is in `command_bridge_sender` (started by `marine_autonomy/launch/operator_core_launch.py:94`), not in `udp_bridge`. `command_bridge_sender` is documented as retransmitting operator commands until acked. Something put it into a state where it kept retransmitting at 370k msg/s despite getting acks back at 48k msg/s. Candidate causes (not yet investigated):\n\n- Retransmit timer too aggressive once a backlog builds in the bridge buffer.\n- Ack-match keyed on a field that desynchronizes after a node restart.\n- The 13:13 nav-stack restart caused command_bridge_sender to lose its ack-tracking state and retry every command in its queue.\n\n### Implications for this issue\n\n- The proposed fix here — \"make `marine/response` unidirectional in bridge config\" — would change nothing, because both directions are already unidirectional.\n- The audit conclusion (\"other topics with the same pattern\") is empty — there are no other bidirectional topics to look at.\n- The underlying retransmission-storm bug is real and worth fixing, but it lives in `command_bridge_sender`, not in `udp_bridge` config.\n\nWill dig further into `command_bridge_sender` before filing a follow-up.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null},{"type":6,"timestamp":1779286329,"nonce":"aPSbngshlv+rQ4f9cI78TGfNk/c=","metadata":{"github-id":"UCE_lALOK4fCvM8AAAABDCkoQM6-3gpu"},"target":"25d59d0140dccf6bc2d2ec8f824c05371bb2c31afa8ade5da98d7d92394dfcfa","message":"## Audit + operator-side cross-check refutes the bidirectional-config root cause\n\n\u003e **Correction (2026-05-20, after further analysis):** The \"messages_per_second\" numbers in the tables below came from `TopicStatistics` and substantially overstate actual wire traffic — the bridge counts every local-subscriber callback, every per-destination send attempt (including rate-limited drops), and every retransmission as a separate \"message.\" Actual `sendto()` rates from `RemoteConnection.message.success/failed_bytes_per_second` were ~30× smaller on the operator side. Updated wire-rate section is at the bottom. The audit finding (no bidirectional config) is unaffected; the directional emphasis is.\n\nDid the audit suggested in this issue's last section (\"other bridged topics in `bizzyboat.yaml` should be audited for similar bidirectional-when-it-shouldn't-be patterns\"). Pulled the operator-side bag to cross-check. Result: the storm is real but the mechanism in this issue is wrong.\n\n### Audit: no bidirectional bridge config exists\n\n- `bizzyboat_project11/config/bizzyboat.yaml` (boat bridge, `name: bizzy`): ~40 outbound topics including `command_response: {source: marine/response}`.\n- `bizzyboat_project11/config/operator.yaml` (operator bridge, `name: operator`): **2 outbound topics**: `command: {source: /bizzy/marine/command}`, `joystick_helm: {source: /bizzy/piloting_mode/manual/helm}`.\n- `marine/response` does not appear in `operator.yaml` and never has — git log on that file shows commits `9985192`, `060bd08`, `c030136`, none of which reference `marine/response`.\n- The two `topics_list` blocks are **disjoint**: no topic is bridged in both directions.\n\nThe opening of this issue cites operator-side config `command_response: {source: marine/response}` mirroring the boat side. That config does not exist.\n\n### Per-topic stats during storm (TopicStatistics — inflated, see correction above)\n\nPer-topic peaks from `/operator/udp_bridge/topic_statistics` and `/operator/udp_bridge/remotes/bizzy/topic_statistics` during 17:12–17:35 UTC. **These are bridge-internal \"messages handled\" counters, not wire packets — see wire-rate section below.**\n\n**Operator → boat (per-topic accounting):**\n\n| source_topic → dest_topic | conn | msg/s (stats) | MB/s (stats) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `/bizzy/marine/command` → same | wifi | 370,679 | 29.6 |\n| `/bizzy/marine/command` → same | vpn  | 370,679 | 29.6 |\n| `/bizzy/marine/command` (init) | —    |  99,317 |  8.0 |\n| `/bizzy/piloting_mode/manual/helm` | wifi | 38 | \u003c0.01 |\n\n**Boat → operator (per-topic accounting):**\n\n| source_topic → dest_topic | conn | msg/s (stats) | MB/s (stats) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `/bizzy/marine/response` → same | wifi | 48,991 | 2.74 |\n| `/bizzy/marine/response` → same | vpn  | 48,991 | 2.74 |\n| `/bizzy/marine/response` (init) | —    | 34,153 | 1.91 |\n| `/bizzy/marine/status/mission_manager` | wifi | 448 | 0.06 |\n\n### Actual wire traffic (RemoteConnection success+failed bytes)\n\n`udp_bridge.cpp:383` sets `SendResult::success` only after `sendto()` returns the full byte count. `SendResult::dropped` (lines 210/322) is set by the rate-limiter *before* `sendto()`. So `success + failed` = bytes the bridge actually pushed into the UDP socket. From `RemoteConnection.message.{success,failed}_bytes_per_second` summed over connections in the bag's `bridge_info` messages:\n\n| Side | Wire out (`sendto`'d) | Wire in (received) |\n|---|---|---|\n| operator | **20 KB/s** | 370 KB/s |\n| bizzy    | **1.8 MB/s** | 10 KB/s |\n\nSo:\n- Operator wire-out was essentially idle (20 KB/s) — not the 60 MB/s the per-topic stats imply.\n- Bizzy wire-out was 1.8 MB/s, saturating its budget (1.5 MB/s wifi + 1.0 MB/s vpn).\n- ~80 % packet loss on the bizzy→operator path during storm (operator received only 0.37 MB/s of bizzy's 1.8 MB/s).\n\n**The directional reading is now the opposite of what this issue's tables suggested**: bizzy was the loud side, not the operator. The operator was nearly silent on the wire.\n\n### Likely real mechanism (revised)\n\n`command_bridge_sender` (started by `marine_autonomy/launch/operator_core_launch.py:94`) has a 1 Hz timer publishing one msg per queued cmd; the queue is keyed by command name (~10 distinct cmds in `command_bridge_receiver.py`). Per source code, max output is ~10 msg/s. The 99 k–370 k figures in the per-topic stats can't be produced by this code as written — they're inflated by the bridge's accounting (1 add per destination × per retransmission attempt × per rate-limited drop).\n\nWhat's actually true:\n- Boat-side `command_bridge_receiver.command_callback` acks **every** received command (line 94, intentional, \"in case the previous ack was lost\") — so ack rate equals received-command rate, including dups.\n- Bizzy's 1.8 MB/s on the wire was real, but how much of it was `marine/response` vs other topics needs to be derived from `RemoteConnection.message.success_bytes_per_second` per-topic on the boat side. The per-topic msg/s claim of 48 k for `marine/response` is also stats-inflated.\n\nThe trigger correlates with the 13:13 nav-stack restart, but the specific mechanism (what put `command_bridge_sender` into a state where it published faster than 10 msg/s, or what other path generated the elevated boat-side outbound) is not yet pinned.\n\n### Implications for this issue\n\n- \"Make `marine/response` unidirectional in bridge config\" — config is already unidirectional both ways. The fix wouldn't change anything.\n- Audit conclusion: there are no bidirectional bridge entries to flag.\n- The \"~150 k UDP packets/s of bridge traffic\" claim in the issue body is inflated stats accounting, not wire packets. The wire was capped at the configured 1.5 + 1.0 MB/s by the rate-limiter, working as intended.\n- **The per-topic `messages_per_second` overstating wire activity by 30× on operator is itself a diagnostic-UX issue worth filing**, since operator panels reading those stats would have wildly misled an oncall watching during the event.\n- The underlying \"something generated elevated traffic during the nav-restart window\" is real and worth investigating, but should be re-scoped against the wire rates, not the inflated per-topic stats.\n\nContinuing to dig before filing a follow-up.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null},{"type":3,"timestamp":1779287381,"nonce":"SgVuI4yNuNOOGeCEKUnQ4z7u60M=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABDC-dFw","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/144#issuecomment-4499414295"},"message":"## Cross-deployment comparison — not a regression, normal link-loss behavior\n\nContinuing from the prior correction comment. The question now is whether the 13:13 storm represents a regression in `udp_bridge` between 2026-05-01 and 2026-05-19, or just a normal-but-visible link-loss event.\n\n### `udp_bridge` changes between the two deployments\n\nMultiple resend-related fixes landed in `rolker/udp_bridge` between 2026-05-01 and 2026-05-19 (issue #9 series):\n\n- `00f282d` exponential backoff + TTL-bounded give-up for resend requests\n- `0eff305` debounce missing-packet detection in `getMissingPackets`\n- `abdb3b0` align sender TTL with receiver window + add `resend_giveup_count`\n- `ff32f58` stop false-give-up on recovered packets\n- `8c9b21d` prevent re-request after give-up + clamp backoff shift\n\nSo the resend logic is materially different between deployments — the suspect surface is large enough that \"regression\" needed to be ruled out.\n\n### Pattern under wifi link loss — 2026-05-01 vs 2026-05-19\n\nOn 2026-05-01 there was no wifi power-off — the boat just went out of wifi range during normal operation. Found multiple windows where `bridge_info.received_bytes_per_second` from operator dropped to ~0 on wifi while bizzy's outbound stayed high. Same pattern as 2026-05-19, before and after the resend-logic changes.\n\n**Top-5 highest-resend samples per deployment (boat-side `bridge_info`):**\n\n**2026-05-01:**\n\n| t (UTC) | wifi wire-out | wifi resend% | vpn wire-out | vpn resend% |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 19:24:18 | 2.29 MB/s | 75% | 1.31 MB/s | 71% |\n| 18:30:56 | 2.14 MB/s | 68% | 1.48 MB/s | 70% |\n| 19:12:18 | 2.09 MB/s | 67% | 1.52 MB/s | 71% |\n| 20:36:34 | 2.12 MB/s | 68% | 1.45 MB/s | 69% |\n| 18:30:52 | 2.23 MB/s | 65% | 1.48 MB/s | 66% |\n\n**2026-05-19:**\n\n| t (UTC) | wifi wire-out | wifi resend% | vpn wire-out | vpn resend% |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 17:28:30 | 1.38 MB/s | 65% | 0.43 MB/s | 20% |\n| 16:58:36 | 1.45 MB/s | 66% | 0.39 MB/s |  3% |\n| 17:06:50 | 1.44 MB/s | 67% | 0.38 MB/s |  2% |\n| 17:07:00 | 1.45 MB/s | 66% | 0.38 MB/s |  2% |\n| 17:10:32 | 1.45 MB/s | 66% | 0.39 MB/s |  3% |\n\n### Reading\n\n- The resend-storm signature is **identical** in shape: when wifi rx≈0, ~65-75% of wifi wire-out becomes resends. By design — udp_bridge is meant to keep trying so it recovers fast when the link returns.\n- 2026-05-19 is **milder** than 2026-05-01: peak wifi wire-out 1.45 MB/s vs 2.29 MB/s; resend ratio 65-67% vs 67-75%. The new resend logic (exponential backoff, TTL-bounded give-up, debounce) appears to be bounding the storm — doing what was intended.\n- The big behavioral difference is on **VPN**: 2026-05-01 had VPN in storm too (70% resends on the vpn side), meaning both links were stressed. 2026-05-19 the VPN was healthy (2-3% resends on vpn carrying ~0.4 MB/s cleanly), only wifi was dead. That's a healthier failure profile.\n- Multiple wifi-loss episodes happened on 2026-05-01 with this exact pattern and **were not noticed at the time** — because the bridge handled them correctly: when wifi came back, traffic resumed.\n\n### Recommendation: close as not-a-bug\n\n- The bidirectional-config root cause this issue cites is wrong (see prior correction comment).\n- The storm magnitude was substantially inflated by `messages_per_second` stats accounting (the bridge counts per-destination attempts + retransmissions as separate \"messages\").\n- The actual wire behavior was identical to 2026-05-01's wifi-out-of-range pattern, milder than that deployment's worst, and within the configured rate limits (1.5 MB/s wifi + 1.0 MB/s vpn).\n- No regression in `udp_bridge` between deployments; if anything the new logic improved bounding.\n\nThe remaining real signal worth tracking separately:\n\n- **Stats-vs-wire diagnostic UX**: per-topic `messages_per_second` overstating wire activity 30× makes operator panels misleading during link loss. Worth a small follow-up in `rolker/udp_bridge` (clarify field semantics, add a wire-rate companion, or filter the per-topic stats to wire-bytes only). The original alarm-bell in the dev log came from reading the inflated msg/s.\n\nIf there's agreement, I'll close this issue with a final comment pointing to those follow-ups.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}